pragma language_version >= 0.16 && <= 0.23;

import CompactStandardLibrary;

// ============================================================================
// ELIGIBILITY GATE + DARKVEIL PSM (merged) — Noctis Zone, Cardano Launch
// ============================================================================
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TWIN RULE — read before changing anything in the DarkVeil half of this file
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This contract and bonding_curve.compact (Midnight Launch) are twins: both
// merge DarkVeil registration, bonding and commit/reveal buying into one
// contract, and both
// carry near-identical circuits for it. They drifted once, because a fix
// applied to one was not applied to the other, and the difference was only
// visible to someone reading both files side by side.
//
// So: any change to a DarkVeil circuit here is either applied in the twin as
// well, or explicitly declined in writing — a comment at the changed circuit
// saying which tier it does not apply to and why. "I only needed it here" is
// how the drift happened; a stated reason is checkable, silence is not.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is Cardano Launch's merged DarkVeil contract — eligibility_gate.compact
// (allowlist, cumulative cap, NIGHT bond lock/refund) folded together with
// darkveil.compact (private commit/reveal buying, ZK Fair Launch Cert).
// Cardano Launch's public bonding curve moved to Cardano/Aiken (resolved,
// contracts/cardano/bonding_curve_tier_b.ak) — this contract only ever
// covers the private DarkVeil pre-sale phase, never the public phase.
//
// MERGED (Phase 2 security-audit fix, 2026-07-11), mirroring the exact
// reasoning already established for Midnight Launch's bonding_curve.compact:
// Compact has no working cross-contract call mechanism (verified against
// the real compiler — every call form fails with "contract types are not
// yet implemented"), so a Cardano Launch registrant's real per-registrant
// DarkVeil purchase total (needed for a ratio-based partial bond refund)
// couldn't be read by this file while it lived in a separate deployed
// darkveil.compact. Folding both into one deployed contract with one
// shared ledger is the only mechanism confirmed to work. This closes two
// gaps in the same pass:
//   1. Cardano Launch previously had NO ratio-based partial bond refund at all —
//      only the full-refund claimBondRefund (cancelled/failed launches).
//      A registrant who bought less than their full allocation in a
//      normally-closed DarkVeil had zero on-chain path to reclaim any
//      bond portion. claimRatioBondRefund below ports Midnight Launch's already-
//      proven ratio-refund formula, now that dvTokensPurchased/baseSlot live
//      in the same contract as lockedBonds.
//   2. Bonus fix found in the same pass: revealBuyCommit never updated
//      cumulativePurchases at all (checkAndUpdateCap was a standalone
//      circuit nothing else in this file called) — the 5% cumulative cap
//      had no enforced link to an actual DarkVeil purchase. Now wired in
//      atomically, same pattern Midnight Launch's merge already uses. That standalone
//      checkAndUpdateCap circuit was later removed from this file entirely
//      (2026-07-12) once nothing called it here either — see
//      submitBuyCommit's own comment below for what replaced it.
//
// Standalone darkveil.compact is retired as of this merge — Cardano Launch now
// deploys only this one contract for the whole DarkVeil phase. See
// internal tracking / commit history for the migration note.
//
// RESOLVED (2026-07-11): Cardano Launch's DarkVeil buy is ADA-denominated,
// and ADA isn't representable as a Midnight-native unshielded token, so
// revealBuyCommit here was never going to be able to enforce payment
// directly (and still doesn't — that's by design now, not a gap). Real
// settlement (ADA payment + token delivery) moved entirely to Cardano's
// `contracts/cardano/bonding_curve_tier_b.ak` via a new permissionless
// `ClaimDarkVeilTokens` redeemer, using a privacy-preserving Merkle-root
// claim (replacing an earlier plaintext cap pre-seed that had also leaked
// individual buy amounts — both problems fixed together). revealBuyCommit
// itself needed no change: it's correctly just a private commit/reveal of
// intent, not where real ADA changes hands. See internal tracking's
// entry for the full mechanism.
//
// RESOLVED (2026-07-12): submitBuyCommit also gained a required proof
// of prior DarkVeil registration (a recomputed registration nullifier
// checked against the already-public registrationNullifiers set) — before
// this, any wallet could submit a buy commitment without ever registering,
// bypassing the wallet-age/allowlist/NIGHT-bond eligibility gate entirely.
//
// Identity note: eligibility_gate.compact and darkveil.compact previously
// derived the caller's UserPublicKey under TWO DIFFERENT domain-separated
// hashes ("noctis:user:pk:v1" / "noctis:darkveil:user:pk:v1") — fine when
// they were separate contracts, but would silently defeat the point of a
// shared identity space if kept: a registrant's registration identity and
// buy identity would never match as the same map key. This merge
// standardizes on ONE identity derivation (eligibility_gate's original
// "noctis:user:pk:v1"/"noctis:governor:pk:v1" domains) for everything in
// this contract — lockedBonds, cumulativePurchases, registrationNullifiers,
// commitments/buyNullifiers/dvTokensPurchased are all keyed by that same
// identity now, not darkveil's old per-file one.
//
// Privacy model:
//   - Users prove allowlist membership via a ZK witness without revealing
//     which allowlist entry is theirs (Merkle proof pattern).
//   - The user's identity is a witness-derived UserPublicKey (32-byte hash
//     of a private secret), not a raw public key.
//   - Cumulative purchase amounts are tracked on-chain by UserPublicKey,
//     but the mapping from UserPublicKey to real-world identity is unknown.
//   - DarkVeil buy amounts are committed privately; only the aggregate is
//     public until reveal (see PRIVACY ANALYSIS section for the full
//     data-lifecycle breakdown).
//
// Reuses patterns from:
//   - midnight-modules/merkle-membership (allowlist gating)
//   - midnight-modules/commitment-nullifier (double-registration / double-buy prevention)
//   - midnight-modules/access-control (admin/governor pattern)
//   - midnight-expert FungibleToken (witness-derived UserPublicKey)
//   - SilentLedger/AssetVerification (composite key, expiry, replay prevention)
//   - SilentLedger/SilentOrderbook (commitment-based orders, match lifecycle)
//
// Compiler: compactc v0.31.1
// ============================================================================

// ============================================================================
// DATA STRUCTURES
// ============================================================================

export enum LaunchPhase { Pending, DarkVeil, Public, Graduated, Cancelled }

// DarkVeil's own sub-phase within the overall LaunchPhase.DarkVeil period
// (registration vs buying vs closed) — a finer-grained state machine than
// LaunchPhase, tracked separately on purpose, same distinction
// bonding_curve.compact's Midnight Launch merge already uses.
export enum DarkVeilState { Inactive, Registration, Buying, Closed, Cancelled }

export enum CommitStatus { Open, Revealed, Cancelled }

export struct UserSecretKey {
  bytes: Bytes<32>;
}

export struct UserPublicKey {
  bytes: Bytes<32>;
}

export struct AdminPublicKey {
  bytes: Bytes<32>;
}

// Input for computing a registration commitment from private data
// Fix (2026-07-21, Medium): dropped the prover-chosen `nonce`
// field — see computeRegistrationCommit's own comment for why.
// `domain` leads every hashed struct in this file, and every one carries a
// different constant. `persistentHash` hashes a value's ENCODED FIELDS, not
// its type — two structs with the same field types in the same order hash
// identically for the same values, and a `Vector<n, Bytes<32>>` hashes
// identically to an n-field struct of `Bytes<32>`. So a distinct leading
// constant, not the declared type, is what keeps one kind of commitment out
// of another's value space.
struct RegistrationCommitInput {
  domain: Bytes<32>;
  userKey: Bytes<32>;
  launchId: Bytes<32>;
  bondAmount: Uint<128>;
}

// One level of a Merkle inclusion proof — the sibling hash at that level,
// and whether the node being folded up is the LEFT child (true) or RIGHT
// child (false) of the pair being hashed. Fix (2026-07-09).
struct MerkleProofEntry {
  sibling: Bytes<32>;
  goesLeft: Boolean;
}

// Input to a single Merkle tree node hash — domain-separated so this can
// never collide with any other persistentHash use in this contract (or
// elsewhere in the codebase) even if the byte shape happened to match.
struct AllowlistNodeInput {
  domain: Bytes<32>;
  left: Bytes<32>;
  right: Bytes<32>;
}

// Fix (2026-07-30): node-hash input for the REGISTRANT tree (see
// verifyRegistrant's own comment for why this exists) — identical shape to
// AllowlistNodeInput, kept as its own named struct so each Merkle tree in
// this file has one clearly-named hash-domain use, matching this file's
// existing convention (RegistrationCommitInput/BuyCommitInput/
// NullifierInput/BuyNullifierInput are all separate structs too, even
// where field shapes overlap).
struct RegistrantNodeInput {
  domain: Bytes<32>;
  left: Bytes<32>;
  right: Bytes<32>;
}

// --- from darkveil.compact ---

// Private buy commitment — hides the purchase amount and buyer identity
struct BuyCommit {
  commitment: Bytes<32>;    // Hash of (buyerKey, amount, price, nonce)
  nullifier: Bytes<32>;     // Unique per (buyerKey, launchId) — prevents double-buy
  timestamp: Uint<64>;      // When the commitment was placed
  status: CommitStatus;     // Open, Revealed, Cancelled
}

// Input for computing buy commitment from private data
struct BuyCommitInput {
  domain: Bytes<32>;
  buyerKey: Bytes<32>;
  launchId: Bytes<32>;
  tokenAmount: Uint<128>;
  pricePerToken: Uint<128>;
  nonce: Bytes<32>;
}

// Input for computing the REAL buy nullifier (fix, 2026-07-21).
struct BuyNullifierInput {
  domain: Bytes<32>;
  buyerSecret: Bytes<32>;
  launchId: Bytes<32>;
}

// ZK Fair Launch Certificate data
export struct FairLaunchCert {
  launchId: Bytes<32>;
  totalParticipants: Uint<64>;
  totalTokensAllocated: Uint<128>;
  totalRaised: Uint<128>;
  participationRate: Uint<8>;   // Percentage of allowlist that participated
  closeTimestamp: Uint<64>;
  certHash: Bytes<32>;          // Unique certificate hash
}

// Input for certificate hash computation
struct CertHashInput {
  domain: Bytes<32>;
  launchId: Bytes<32>;
  totalParticipants: Uint<64>;
  totalTokensAllocated: Uint<128>;
  totalRaised: Uint<128>;
  closeTimestamp: Uint<64>;
}

// ============================================================================
// LEDGER STATE
// ============================================================================

// The launch identifier (set at deploy time, seals this gate to one launch)
sealed ledger launchId: Bytes<32>;

// Merkle root of the allowlist (governor-updatable for late additions)
export ledger allowlistRoot: Bytes<32>;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THRESHOLD ATTESTATION — the allowlist takes two signers, not one
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// allowlistRoot decides who may take part in DarkVeil at all, and no circuit
// here can check it: eligibility is computed off-chain against Cardano history
// that no Midnight circuit can read. So it is attested, and it takes two of
// three attestors in SEPARATE calls.
//
// Same reasoning as cto_governance's balance snapshot: a threshold checked
// inside one circuit is one party holding every secret, because every witness
// for a call is produced by whoever builds that call. Approvals are keyed by
// signer, and rounds are numbered rather than cleared so an approval from an
// abandoned round can never be counted toward the current one.
sealed ledger allowlistAttestor1: Bytes<32>;

sealed ledger allowlistAttestor2: Bytes<32>;

sealed ledger allowlistAttestor3: Bytes<32>;

sealed ledger allowlistThreshold: Uint<8>;

sealed ledger allowlistExpirySeconds: Uint<64>;

export ledger pendingAllowlistRoot: Bytes<32>;

export ledger pendingAllowlistOpenedAt: Uint<64>;

export ledger allowlistRound: Counter;

export ledger allowlistApprovals: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<64>>;

// Fix (2026-07-30): Merkle root of the REGISTRANT tree — every
// wallet that successfully registered for DarkVeil, published once by the
// governor at startBuying() (the registration freeze, where the
// registrant set becomes fixed for the round). submitBuyCommit proves
// membership against this root via a real Merkle proof (verifyRegistrant,
// mirroring verifyAllowlist's own construction) instead of the old
// registrationNullifiers-membership check, which was publicly
// precomputable — caller.bytes is already disclosed via lockedBonds at
// registration, and launchId/bondAmount are both public constants, so
// anyone could recompute a registrant's expected nullifier value ahead of
// time and match it against a later submitBuyCommit's disclosed nullifier,
// de-anonymizing which registrant was buying. A ZK membership proof
// reveals only "some leaf in the published tree matches," never which
// one — closing the gap for real, not just changing the hash formula (see
// this file's history / local/SECURITY_AUDIT.md's entry for the full
// analysis of why a second nullifier alone would not have been sufficient).
// Meaningless until startBuying sets it (mirrors baseSlot's own comment).
export ledger registrantRoot: Bytes<32>;

// Current phase of the launch
export ledger phase: LaunchPhase;

// Per-user cumulative purchase amount (in token units, across all phases).
// Only the DarkVeil-phase portion is actually enforced here now (via
// revealBuyCommit, wired in by this merge) — the public phase lives on
// Cardano and enforces its own copy of the cap there, pre-seeded
// from this map via the relayer/anchor mechanism (see CLAUDE.md's Cardano Launch
// cap description).
export ledger cumulativePurchases: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

// Total supply of the launch token (for 5% cap calculation)
sealed ledger totalSupply: Uint<128>;

// Maximum per-wallet percentage (5 = 5%)
sealed ledger maxWalletPercent: Uint<8>;

// NIGHT bond amount required for DarkVeil registration
sealed ledger bondAmount: Uint<128>;

// Track locked bonds by user (refundable on cancellation, or partially on
// a normal DarkVeil close via claimRatioBondRefund)
export ledger lockedBonds: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

// Nullifier set — prevents double-registration (commitment-nullifier pattern)
export ledger registrationNullifiers: Set<Bytes<32>>;

// Admin/governor key (witness-derived, set at deploy time)
sealed ledger governorKey: Bytes<32>;

// Creator's identity, under the same UserPublicKey domain regular
// registrants derive under. CLAUDE.md eligibility check #3
// ("Registrant != creator fee-paying wallet address") — set once at
// deploy from a key computed off-chain.
sealed ledger creatorKey: Bytes<32>;

// Total registrations count
export ledger registrationCount: Counter;

// Fix (2026-07-09): whether DarkVeil itself failed (<50%
// participation). Independent of `phase` — under the DarkVeil-failure resolution, a
// failed DarkVeil converts the launch to a public-only launch (phase
// advances to Public, not Cancelled), so `claimBondRefund` can't gate on
// `phase == Cancelled` alone or DV registrants on a converted launch would
// have no way to reclaim their bond at all.
export ledger dvFailed: Boolean;

// Precomputed wallet cap (set at deploy time to avoid division in circuit —
// see the constructor's comment for the 2026-07-09 fix history)
sealed ledger walletCap: Uint<128>;

// --- from darkveil.compact ---

// DarkVeil's own sub-phase (registration/buying/closed) — see the
// DarkVeilState enum's comment for how this differs from `phase`.
export ledger dvState: DarkVeilState;

// Buy commitments keyed by commitment hash
export ledger commitments: Map<Bytes<32>, BuyCommit>;

// Nullifier set — prevents double-buying during DarkVeil
export ledger buyNullifiers: Set<Bytes<32>>;

// Aggregate stats (public for transparency)
export ledger dvTotalParticipants: Counter;
export ledger totalTokensCommitted: Uint<128>;
export ledger totalRaisedCommitted: Uint<128>;

// DarkVeil allocation (how many tokens are available in this phase).
// Exported so anyone can read it straight from the contract's public state,
// which is what a registrant needs to check their own slot against the
// published allocation without taking the platform's word for it.
export sealed ledger dvAllocation: Uint<128>;

// Price per token during DarkVeil (fixed price, not bonding curve;
// ADA-denominated for Cardano Launch). Exported for the same reason: the price a
// buyer is charged is verifiable from chain state alone. Same shape as
// bonding_curve.compact's basePrice/maxPrice.
export sealed ledger dvPrice: Uint<128>;

// Allowlist size (for participation rate calculation)
sealed ledger allowlistSize: Uint<64>;

// ZK Fair Launch Certificate (set when DarkVeil closes)
export ledger fairLaunchCert: FairLaunchCert;

// Fix (2026-07-30): fixed platform-wide window (30 days) after
// DarkVeil closes during which a buyer must reveal their commitment —
// without this, an unrevealed Open commitment could sit in `commitments`
// forever, unbounded storage growth with no expiry path. Hardcoded, not a
// constructor argument — matches cooldownDuration's own hardcoded
// convention in cto_governance.compact for a platform-wide policy
// constant. See CLAUDE.md's DV_REVEAL_WINDOW_DAYS.
sealed ledger revealWindowSeconds: Uint<64>;

// How long after registrationCloseTime anyone may give up on a DarkVeil phase
// that never finished. Seven days: registration close leaves only the buying
// window and settlement to run, so this is several times the sequence that is
// legitimately left. Hardcoded rather than a constructor argument, matching
// revealWindowSeconds' convention for a platform-wide policy constant — and
// deliberately not governor-chosen, since this is the one deadline a governor
// should not be able to push out.
sealed ledger darkVeilExpirySeconds: Uint<64>;

// Registration close timestamp — stored for off-chain/informational use
// only (not read by any circuit's own logic; DarkVeil failure is a
// governor-triggered call via markDarkVeilFailed, not an automatic
// timeout check, same as before this merge).
sealed ledger registrationCloseTime: Uint<64>;

// Resolution (2026-07-13): minimum absolute registrant count required
// before buying can open. Below this, startBuying() rejects and the
// governor must call cancelDarkVeil() instead (the existing DarkVeil-failure
// path, refundable via claimBondRefund). Defends against two griefing
// vectors that a percentage-only participation check can't catch: (a) a
// creator's associates registering as ghosts to dilute baseSlot for
// legitimate buyers, and (b) an attacker cheaply registering >50% of a
// tiny launch's participants purely to force DV to fail (bonds are
// refunded on failure, so the only real cost is gas). CLAUDE.md:
// MIN_DV_PARTICIPANTS = 15.
sealed ledger minDvParticipants: Uint<64>;

// Equivalent fix: per-registrant DarkVeil token allocation —
// CLAUDE.md's `base_slot = dv_supply / registered_count`. This is a FLAT
// per-capita split, the same value for every registrant, not a map — so
// one ledger field is enough. Starts at 0 (meaningless until DV closes);
// set exactly once by closeDarkVeil, computed off-chain (Compact can't
// divide in-circuit) from `dvAllocation / registrationCount` at that
// point, once the final registrant count is known.
export ledger baseSlot: Uint<128>;

// Cumulative tokens a buyer DECLARED (via revealBuyCommit) they purchased
// DURING DarkVeil specifically. NOT payment-verified — Cardano Launch's real ADA
// payment happens on Cardano via ClaimDarkVeilTokens, and Compact has
// no way to observe that from here. This map is therefore a self-reported
// intent statistic only: used to bound the pool-wide/wallet-cap checks in
// revealBuyCommit itself (which have no direct payout consequence), and for
// display. It must NEVER be used to compute a real payout — see
// settledDvPurchases below, which is what claimRatioBondRefund actually
// reads.
//
// Fix (2026-07-30, security audit): this field used to be what
// claimRatioBondRefund read to compute the NIGHT bond refund. Since
// revealBuyCommit never charges anything, a registrant could declare
// tokenAmount == baseSlot for free and claim a 100% bond refund with zero
// real payment — the exact ghost-registrant forfeiture this bond
// exists to enforce, bypassed for the cost of gas. Fixed by introducing a
// SEPARATE, governor-attested ledger (settledDvPurchases) fed from real,
// observed Cardano ClaimDarkVeilTokens settlements, and pointing the
// refund math at that instead.
export ledger dvTokensPurchased: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

// Governor-attested count of tokens a buyer has REALLY settled for —
// written only by recordDarkVeilSettlement, from real, observed Cardano
// ClaimDarkVeilTokens transactions (that mechanism is what actually moves
// ADA and delivers tokens for Cardano Launch; this ledger is Midnight's mirror of
// that real event, not a duplicate of the unpaid declaration above). This
// is what claimRatioBondRefund uses to compute the real refund — same
// governor-published-attestation trust model already used everywhere else
// on this platform (allowlist root, CTO balance-snapshot root, staking
// stake-snapshot root): trust the governor's off-chain computation from
// real, independently-auditable on-chain data, not a caller's own claim.
export ledger settledDvPurchases: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

// The real unshielded address (not a derived identity) forfeited bond NIGHT
// is paid to, set once at deploy.
//
// ONE address. The platform previously ran a treasury wallet and an ops
// wallet and split every fee 60/40 between them; that split is gone
// everywhere, so there is a single destination and no share arithmetic to
// verify. A forfeited bond is paid here in full.
sealed ledger platformAddr: Bytes<32>;

// Set once by finalizeDvSettlement, after the Cardano claim window has closed
// and every real settlement has been recorded. It is what separates "not
// claimed yet" from "did not claim": before it, a bond may still be settled
// against and must not be swept; after it, recordDarkVeilSettlement is
// refused, so the record a sweep reads can no longer change underneath it.
export ledger settlementFinalized: Boolean;

// Registrants who have said, on chain, that they hold a bond with no path in
// registrantRoot — mapped to the time from which they may take that bond back.
// See the REGISTRANT EXCLUSION DISPUTE section for what this is for.
export ledger disputedExclusions: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<64>>;

// Disputes answered by a real inclusion proof. Membership here is permanent
// and is what separates a registrant the tree does contain — whose bond
// forfeits on the ordinary terms — from one it does not. Kept as its own set
// rather than by clearing the dispute, so an answered dispute cannot simply be
// reopened and made to cost another answer.
export ledger rebuttedExclusions: Set<Bytes<32>>;

// How long an unanswered dispute must stand before the bond behind it can be
// taken back: 72 hours, the same window a governance challenge already runs
// for in cto_governance.compact. Hardcoded rather than a constructor argument,
// matching revealWindowSeconds' own convention for a platform-wide policy
// constant.
sealed ledger exclusionDisputeWindow: Uint<64>;

// ============================================================================
// WITNESSES
// ============================================================================

// User's private secret key — never revealed on-chain
witness getUserSecret(): UserSecretKey;

// Merkle proof for allowlist membership — private, stays with prover.
// Fix (2026-07-09): now carries a direction bit per level (needed to
// know whether to hash(node, sibling) or hash(sibling, node) — the old
// Vector<32, Bytes<32>> shape had no way to express this at all).
// Depth reduced 32 -> 20 (2026-07-12): a fixed-depth proof always
// walks every level regardless of real registrant count, so depth is a
// direct proving-cost lever, not just a capacity ceiling. 2^20 (1,048,576)
// registrants is far beyond any realistic DarkVeil round (bond + wallet-age
// gated), for 37.5% fewer hash operations per proof than the original 32.
witness getMerkleProof(): Vector<20, MerkleProofEntry>;

// Fix (2026-07-30): Merkle proof for REGISTRANT tree membership —
// private, stays with the prover. Same shape/depth as getMerkleProof
// (MerkleProofEntry, 20 levels) but a distinct witness, since it proves
// membership in a different tree (registrantRoot, not allowlistRoot) built
// at a different time (registration freeze, not deploy) — see
// verifyRegistrant's own comment.
witness getRegistrantMerkleProof(): Vector<20, MerkleProofEntry>;

// Fix (2026-07-30): getRegistrationNonce (a dead witness — nothing
// in this file called it since the 2026-07-21 fix dropped the
// nonce from computeRegistrationCommit) was removed here — a real footgun
// for whoever wires the production PrivateStateProvider, since a declared
// witness signals the contract genuinely needs that private input.

// Governor's secret key (for admin circuits)
witness getGovernorSecret(): UserSecretKey;

// Random nonce for a DarkVeil buy commitment — private, prevents replay
witness getBuyNonce(): Bytes<32>;

// ============================================================================
// HELPER CIRCUITS
// ============================================================================

// Derive user public key from secret (domain-separated, same pattern as
// FungibleToken). ONE identity for this whole merged contract now — see
// file header's identity note.
// A registrant's identity is scoped to ONE launch: the same secret derives a
// different key under every launchId, so the key that registers here cannot
// be recognised as the same person registering for another launch. `launchId`
// is sealed, so a launch cannot move a registrant's identity after deployment.
pure circuit deriveUserPublicKey(sk: UserSecretKey, launchId_: Bytes<32>): UserPublicKey {
  return UserPublicKey {
    bytes: persistentHash<Vector<3, Bytes<32>>>([
      pad(32, "noctis:user:pk:v1"),
      sk.bytes,
      launchId_
    ])
  };
}

// Derive admin/governor public key from secret (domain-separated)
pure circuit deriveGovernorKey(sk: UserSecretKey): AdminPublicKey {
  return AdminPublicKey {
    bytes: persistentHash<Vector<2, Bytes<32>>>([
      pad(32, "noctis:governor:pk:v1"),
      sk.bytes
    ])
  };
}

// Check if a user key is the zero key (invalid)
pure circuit isKeyZero(key: UserPublicKey): Boolean {
  return key == default<UserPublicKey>;
}

// Compute registration commitment from private data
// Fix (2026-07-21, Medium): previously took a prover-chosen
// `nonce`, making re-registration possible — each call with a fresh nonce
// produced a fresh nullifier that passed the "already registered" check,
// and lockedBonds.insert overwrites (rather than accumulates) per-caller,
// silently stranding every duplicate bond beyond the last one written.
// Dropped the nonce entirely: this is now deterministic per (userKey,
// launch, bond) — since bondAmount is a fixed platform constant for a
// given launch, a single secret can only ever produce ONE registration
// nullifier, closing the reuse path the same way computeBuyNullifier
// closed the parallel buy-side bug.
pure circuit computeRegistrationCommit(
  userKey: Bytes<32>,
  launch: Bytes<32>,
  bond: Uint<128>
): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<RegistrationCommitInput>(RegistrationCommitInput {
    domain: pad(32, "noctis:dv:reg:commit:v1"),
    userKey: userKey,
    launchId: launch,
    bondAmount: bond
  });
}

// --- from darkveil.compact (identity unified, see file header) ---

// Compute buy commitment from private data
pure circuit computeBuyCommit(
  buyerKey: Bytes<32>,
  launch: Bytes<32>,
  tokenAmount: Uint<128>,
  pricePerToken: Uint<128>,
  nonce: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<BuyCommitInput>(BuyCommitInput {
    domain: pad(32, "noctis:dv:buy:commit:v1"),
    buyerKey: buyerKey,
    launchId: launch,
    tokenAmount: tokenAmount,
    pricePerToken: pricePerToken,
    nonce: nonce
  });
}

// Fix (2026-07-21, Compact security-review pass — High): the buy
// nullifier used to be a free caller-supplied Bytes<32> parameter on
// submitBuyCommit, checked only for prior use (!buyNullifiers.member) —
// never verified to actually derive from the caller's own identity. That
// let a single registrant submit unlimited buy commitments (a different
// nullifier each call), capturing up to walletCap (5%) instead of their
// fair per-registrant share. Separately, the original public-key-derived
// nullifier formula (buyerKey + launchId, both public — buyerKey is
// disclosed via lockedBonds/cumulativePurchases map keys, and launchId is
// sealed/public) let anyone precompute any registrant's nullifier,
// breaking the commitment-unlinkability this file's own PRIVACY ANALYSIS
// claims. (Fix, 2026-07-30: that original formula, computeNullifier/
// NullifierInput, was dead code by this point — kept only as a documented
// hash helper — and has since been removed; this comment is preserved for
// the historical reasoning, not a still-live reference.)
// Fixed by deriving the buy nullifier from the caller's SECRET key bytes
// (never disclosed anywhere in this contract) instead of their public
// derived key: a single secret can only ever produce ONE nullifier value
// per launch (closing the unlimited-resubmission bug — there is no
// attacker-choosable nonce component to vary), and nobody without that
// exact secret can precompute it (closing the precomputability leak).
// Domain-separated from computeRegistrationCommit's own domain so a
// registration nullifier and a buy nullifier for the same secret can
// never collide.
pure circuit computeBuyNullifier(
  buyerSecret: Bytes<32>,
  launch: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<BuyNullifierInput>(BuyNullifierInput {
    domain: pad(32, "noctis:dv:buy:nullifier:v1"),
    buyerSecret: buyerSecret,
    launchId: launch
  });
}

// Compute certificate hash
pure circuit computeCertHash(
  launch: Bytes<32>,
  participants: Uint<64>,
  tokensAllocated: Uint<128>,
  raised: Uint<128>,
  closeTime: Uint<64>
): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<CertHashInput>(CertHashInput {
    domain: pad(32, "noctis:dv:cert:v1"),
    launchId: launch,
    totalParticipants: participants,
    totalTokensAllocated: tokensAllocated,
    totalRaised: raised,
    closeTimestamp: closeTime
  });
}

// Verify a claimed ratio-based NIGHT bond refund is the FLOOR of
// `bonded * purchased / allocated` — same formula and floor-rounding
// double-inequality pattern as bonding_curve.compact's Midnight Launch copy.
// purchased == allocated -> refund == bonded (100% back); purchased == 0
// -> refund == 0 (ghost, fully forfeited); anything between is the
// proportional partial return.
// Phase 5 hygiene fix (2026-07-12): marked pure — depends only on its own
// parameters, no ledger or witness access.
pure circuit verifyRatioRefund(
  bonded: Uint<128>,
  purchased: Uint<128>,
  allocated: Uint<128>,
  claimedRefund: Uint<128>
): Boolean {
  assert(disclose(allocated > 0), "allocated must be positive"); // baseSlot must have been set
  assert(disclose(purchased <= allocated), "purchased cannot exceed allocated");
  // Note (2026-07-21, Low): this 2^44-1 (~17.6 trillion atomic
  // units) range cap is a real ceiling on `bonded` (NIGHT_BOND_USD's
  // atomic-unit equivalent) — if `bondAmount` is ever configured above it
  // at deploy, every claimRatioBondRefund call would abort here,
  // permanently bricking bond refunds for that launch. Not expected in
  // practice (bondAmount is a small, platform-controlled USD-equivalent
  // constant, nowhere near this ceiling), but worth checking against
  // whatever value is actually deployed if NIGHT_BOND_USD or its
  // conversion rate ever changes materially.
  assert(disclose(bonded <= 17592186044415), "bonded exceeds safe range"); // 2^44 - 1
  assert(disclose(purchased <= 1099511627775), "purchased exceeds safe range"); // 2^40 - 1
  assert(disclose(allocated <= 1099511627775), "allocated exceeds safe range"); // 2^40 - 1
  assert(disclose(claimedRefund <= 17592186044415), "claimedRefund exceeds safe range"); // 2^44 - 1

  const bondedN = bonded as Uint<44>;
  const purchasedN = purchased as Uint<40>;
  const allocatedN = allocated as Uint<40>;
  const claimedRefundN = claimedRefund as Uint<44>;

  const bondedTimesPurchased = ((bondedN as Uint<84>) * (purchasedN as Uint<84>)) as Uint<84>;
  const claimedTimesAllocated = ((claimedRefundN as Uint<84>) * (allocatedN as Uint<84>)) as Uint<84>;
  const nextClaimedTimesAllocated = ((claimedTimesAllocated as Uint<85>) + (allocatedN as Uint<85>)) as Uint<85>;

  return disclose(
    (claimedTimesAllocated as Uint<85>) <= (bondedTimesPurchased as Uint<85>)
      && (bondedTimesPurchased as Uint<85>) < nextClaimedTimesAllocated
  );
}

// ============================================================================
// CONSTRUCTOR
// ============================================================================

constructor(
  launchId_: Bytes<32>,
  allowlistRoot_: Bytes<32>,
  totalSupply_: Uint<128>,
  maxWalletPercent_: Uint<8>,
  bondAmount_: Uint<128>,
  walletCap_: Uint<128>,
  dvAllocation_: Uint<128>,
  dvPrice_: Uint<128>,
  allowlistSize_: Uint<64>,
  registrationCloseTime_: Uint<64>,
  minDvParticipants_: Uint<64>,
  creatorPubKey_: Bytes<32>,
  platformAddr_: Bytes<32>,
  // The three keys that may attest the allowlist, and how many it takes.
  allowlistAttestor1_: Bytes<32>,
  allowlistAttestor2_: Bytes<32>,
  allowlistAttestor3_: Bytes<32>,
  allowlistThreshold_: Uint<8>
) {
  launchId = disclose(launchId_);
  allowlistRoot = disclose(allowlistRoot_);
  // Three distinct, non-empty keys or the threshold is decorative: two equal
  // keys let one holder supply both halves of a 2-of-3.
  assert(disclose(allowlistAttestor1_ != pad(32, "")), "allowlistAttestor1 cannot be empty");
  assert(disclose(allowlistAttestor2_ != pad(32, "")), "allowlistAttestor2 cannot be empty");
  assert(disclose(allowlistAttestor3_ != pad(32, "")), "allowlistAttestor3 cannot be empty");
  assert(disclose(allowlistAttestor1_ != allowlistAttestor2_), "allowlist attestor keys must be distinct");
  assert(disclose(allowlistAttestor1_ != allowlistAttestor3_), "allowlist attestor keys must be distinct");
  assert(disclose(allowlistAttestor2_ != allowlistAttestor3_), "allowlist attestor keys must be distinct");
  assert(disclose(allowlistThreshold_ >= 2 && allowlistThreshold_ <= 3), "allowlistThreshold must be 2 or 3");
  allowlistAttestor1 = disclose(allowlistAttestor1_);
  allowlistAttestor2 = disclose(allowlistAttestor2_);
  allowlistAttestor3 = disclose(allowlistAttestor3_);
  allowlistThreshold = disclose(allowlistThreshold_);
  allowlistExpirySeconds = disclose(86400); // 24 hours
  pendingAllowlistRoot = disclose(pad(32, ""));
  pendingAllowlistOpenedAt = disclose(0);
  registrantRoot = disclose(pad(32, "")); // meaningless until startBuying sets it
  totalSupply = disclose(totalSupply_);
  maxWalletPercent = disclose(maxWalletPercent_);
  // Fix (2026-07-30): verifyRatioRefund's own 2^44-1 ceiling on
  // `bonded` would otherwise only ever be checked the first time
  // claimRatioBondRefund runs — potentially long after registration,
  // bonding, and buying are all already committed for this launch. Fail
  // fast at deploy time instead: if bondAmount is ever configured above
  // this ceiling, no bond refund could ever succeed for the whole launch.
  assert(disclose(bondAmount_ <= 17592186044415), "bondAmount exceeds verifyRatioRefund's safe range"); // 2^44 - 1
  bondAmount = disclose(bondAmount_);

  // walletCap MUST equal totalSupply * maxWalletPercent / 100, computed
  // off-chain (Compact circuits can't divide) and passed in directly.
  // Deploy-time callers must compute this themselves:
  //   walletCap = totalSupply * maxWalletPercent / 100
  assert(disclose(walletCap_ > 0), "walletCap must be positive");
  walletCap = disclose(walletCap_);

  // Set governor from deployer's secret
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  governorKey = disclose(govKey.bytes);
  creatorKey = disclose(creatorPubKey_);
  assert(disclose(platformAddr_ != pad(32, "")), "platformAddr cannot be empty");
  platformAddr = disclose(platformAddr_);
  settlementFinalized = disclose(false);

  phase = LaunchPhase.Pending;
  dvFailed = disclose(false);

  // --- from darkveil.compact ---
  dvAllocation = disclose(dvAllocation_);
  dvPrice = disclose(dvPrice_);
  allowlistSize = disclose(allowlistSize_);
  // A zero anchor would put expireDarkVeil's deadline in the past at
  // deploy, so the launch could be expired before it began.
  assert(disclose(registrationCloseTime_ > 0), "registrationCloseTime must be set");
  registrationCloseTime = disclose(registrationCloseTime_);
  assert(disclose(minDvParticipants_ > 0), "minDvParticipants must be positive");
  minDvParticipants = disclose(minDvParticipants_);
  dvState = DarkVeilState.Inactive;
  totalTokensCommitted = 0;
  totalRaisedCommitted = 0;
  baseSlot = 0; // meaningless until closeDarkVeil sets it
  revealWindowSeconds = disclose(2592000); // 30 days — see ledger comment
  darkVeilExpirySeconds = disclose(604800); // 7 days — see ledger comment
  exclusionDisputeWindow = disclose(259200); // 72 hours — see ledger comment

  fairLaunchCert = disclose(FairLaunchCert {
    launchId: launchId_,
    totalParticipants: 0,
    totalTokensAllocated: 0,
    totalRaised: 0,
    participationRate: 0,
    closeTimestamp: 0,
    certHash: pad(32, "")
  });
}

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — ALLOWLIST VERIFICATION
// ============================================================================

// Verify allowlist membership via a real 20-level Merkle inclusion proof
// (witness-based, privacy-preserving — only the root is public; a user's
// own leaf and path are never revealed to anyone but the prover). Depth
// reduced 32 -> 20, 2026-07-12 — see getMerkleProof's witness
// declaration comment.
//
// Design requirement: the leaf is derived in-circuit from the
// caller's own identity (persistentHash of a domain + caller bytes)
// instead of a free witness value, so a valid proof can only be
// constructed by whoever controls the secret behind `caller`.
circuit verifyAllowlist(caller: Bytes<32>): [] {
  const leaf = persistentHash<Vector<2, Bytes<32>>>([
    pad(32, "noctis:allowlist:leaf:v1"),
    caller
  ]);
  const proof = getMerkleProof();

  const recomputedRoot = fold(
    (acc: Bytes<32>, entry: MerkleProofEntry): Bytes<32> => {
      const left = disclose(entry.goesLeft) ? acc : entry.sibling;
      const right = disclose(entry.goesLeft) ? entry.sibling : acc;
      return persistentHash<AllowlistNodeInput>(AllowlistNodeInput {
        domain: pad(32, "noctis:allowlist:node:v1"),
        left: left,
        right: right
      });
    },
    leaf,
    proof
  );

  assert(disclose(recomputedRoot == allowlistRoot), "Invalid allowlist proof");
}

// Fix (2026-07-30): verify prior DarkVeil registration via a real
// Merkle inclusion proof against registrantRoot — same construction as
// verifyAllowlist above, different tree. Replaces the old
// registrationNullifiers-membership check inside submitBuyCommit, which
// disclosed a value (computeRegistrationCommit(caller.bytes, launchId,
// bondAmount)) that anyone could precompute from already-public
// information (caller.bytes is disclosed via lockedBonds at registration;
// launchId/bondAmount are public constants) — letting an observer
// pre-link a registrant's identity to whichever later submitBuyCommit
// transaction discloses that same value. A ZK membership proof discloses
// only that SOME leaf in the published tree matches, never which one,
// closing the gap for real (see registrantRoot's own ledger comment for
// the full analysis).
circuit verifyRegistrant(caller: Bytes<32>): [] {
  const leaf = persistentHash<Vector<2, Bytes<32>>>([
    pad(32, "noctis:registrant:leaf:v1"),
    caller
  ]);
  const proof = getRegistrantMerkleProof();

  const recomputedRoot = fold(
    (acc: Bytes<32>, entry: MerkleProofEntry): Bytes<32> => {
      const left = disclose(entry.goesLeft) ? acc : entry.sibling;
      const right = disclose(entry.goesLeft) ? entry.sibling : acc;
      return persistentHash<RegistrantNodeInput>(RegistrantNodeInput {
        domain: pad(32, "noctis:registrant:node:v1"),
        left: left,
        right: right
      });
    },
    leaf,
    proof
  );

  assert(disclose(recomputedRoot == registrantRoot), "Invalid registrant proof");
}

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — DARKVEIL REGISTRATION
// ============================================================================

// Register for DarkVeil phase
// 1. Verify allowlist membership
// 2. Verify NIGHT bond is provided
// 3. Lock the bond (refundable on cancellation, or partially via
//    claimRatioBondRefund after a normal close)
// 4. Emit nullifier to prevent double-registration
//
// Takes no arguments: everything it needs is either the caller's own
// witness secret or already sealed on this contract. In particular the
// registration nullifier is derived in-circuit from the caller's identity
// rather than accepted from them, so there is no commitment for a caller
// to supply, get wrong, or have ignored.
export circuit registerForDarkVeil(): [] {
  // Must be in DarkVeil phase
  assert(disclose(phase == LaunchPhase.DarkVeil), "DarkVeil registration not active");
  // Fix (2026-07-21, Medium): `phase` alone was checked, never
  // `dvState` — registration stayed open through Buying/Closed (dvState is
  // a separate sub-phase machine, see its own declaration comment), letting
  // new registrants join after the registration freeze / base_slot
  // computation, breaking the CLAUDE.md-documented fairness model (the
  // registration-freeze invariant assumes the registrant set is fixed once
  // Buying starts).
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Registration), "DarkVeil not in registration sub-phase");

  // Derive caller identity (before verifyAllowlist — the design requirement needs the
  // caller bytes to derive the allowlist leaf)
  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  assert(disclose(!isKeyZero(caller)), "Invalid user key");

  // Verify allowlist membership, bound to this specific caller
  verifyAllowlist(caller.bytes);

  // CLAUDE.md eligibility check #3: registrant != creator
  assert(disclose(caller.bytes != creatorKey), "Creator cannot register for DarkVeil");

  // Compute registration commitment (nullifier) — fix (2026-07-21):
  // no longer takes a nonce, see computeRegistrationCommit's own comment.
  const nullifier = computeRegistrationCommit(
    caller.bytes,
    launchId,
    bondAmount
  );

  // Prevent double-registration (commitment-nullifier pattern)
  // disclose() must wrap the witness-derived argument at the point it
  // reaches the ledger operation, not just the outer boolean expression.
  assert(
    !registrationNullifiers.member(disclose(nullifier)),
    "Already registered for this launch"
  );
  registrationNullifiers.insert(disclose(nullifier));

  // Fix: require the NIGHT bond to actually be paid. receiveUnshielded
  // adds a ledger-enforced constraint that this transaction includes a
  // matching unshielded NIGHT input of exactly bondAmount addressed to
  // this contract.
  receiveUnshielded(nativeToken(), disclose(bondAmount));

  // Lock the NIGHT bond
  lockedBonds.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose(bondAmount));

  // Track registration count
  registrationCount.increment(1);
}

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — CUMULATIVE CAP TRACKING
// ============================================================================

// Phase 4 fix (2026-07-12): the standalone checkAndUpdateCap circuit that
// used to live here was removed — nothing in this contract ever called it
// (revealBuyCommit enforces the identical 5% cumulative wallet cap inline,
// see its own "Bonus fix (this merge)" comment below), and no off-chain
// caller invoked it either. Dead code duplicating the real enforcement
// path, same class of cleanup as bonding_curve.compact's Midnight Launch merge
// . Test coverage for the cap boundary ported onto revealBuyCommit
// directly — see eligibility_gate.test.ts's "wallet cap enforcement via
// revealBuyCommit" describe block.

// A wallet's cumulative purchases are read from `cumulativePurchases` in the
// contract's public state directly. An exported circuit for that read would
// cost a verifier key on chain and a proven transaction per lookup to return
// a number the indexer already serves for free.

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — BOND REFUND (DarkVeil failure path)
// ============================================================================

// Claim a FULL NIGHT bond refund — launch was cancelled outright, OR
// DarkVeil itself failed and the launch converted to public. For a
// DarkVeil that closed NORMALLY (succeeded), see
// claimRatioBondRefund below instead.
//
// Security-audit fix (Phase 2, 2026-07-11): this used to only clear the
// ledger entry with a comment deferring to "the Zswap layer via
// transaction merging" — a mechanism that doesn't exist. Now takes a
// recipientAddr and pays out via sendUnshielded, mirroring
// bonding_curve.compact's already-fixed Midnight Launch copy of this exact circuit.
export circuit claimBondRefund(recipientAddr: Bytes<32>): [] {
  assert(
    disclose(phase == LaunchPhase.Cancelled || dvFailed),
    "Launch not cancelled and DarkVeil did not fail"
  );

  // Derive caller identity
  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  assert(disclose(!isKeyZero(caller)), "Invalid user key");
  // Phase 5 hygiene fix (2026-07-12): sanity-check the caller-supplied
  // payout destination isn't the empty/all-zero address — guards against a
  // fat-fingered recipientAddr silently burning the refund.
  assert(disclose(recipientAddr != pad(32, "")), "Recipient address cannot be empty");

  // Check caller has a locked bond
  assert(
    lockedBonds.member(disclose(caller.bytes)),
    "No locked bond found for caller"
  );

  const bond = lockedBonds.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes));
  assert(disclose(bond > 0), "Bond already claimed");

  // Clear the bond entry before paying out (checks-effects-interactions).
  lockedBonds.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose(0));

  const dest = right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(disclose(UserAddress { bytes: recipientAddr }));
  sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), disclose(bond), dest);
}

// Claim a RATIO-BASED partial NIGHT bond refund — for a DarkVeil that
// closed NORMALLY (succeeded, not cancelled, not marked failed). Phase 2
// security-audit fix, 2026-07-11: Cardano Launch had no equivalent of this circuit
// at all before this merge (Midnight Launch's bonding_curve.compact has had it
// since its own ratio-refund fix) — a registrant who bought less than their full baseSlot
// allocation had no on-chain path to reclaim any bond portion. Ported
// verbatim from that already-proven implementation, now that
// dvTokensPurchased/baseSlot live in the same contract as lockedBonds.
// CLAUDE.md: `NIGHT_returned = NIGHT_bonded * tokens_purchased / tokens_allocated`.
export circuit claimRatioBondRefund(
  recipientAddr: Bytes<32>,
  claimedRefund: Uint<128>
): [] {
  assert(
    disclose(phase != LaunchPhase.Cancelled && !dvFailed && dvState == DarkVeilState.Closed),
    "Only claimable after a normally-closed, non-cancelled, non-failed DarkVeil"
  );

  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  assert(disclose(!isKeyZero(caller)), "Invalid user key");
  // Phase 5 hygiene fix: same empty-recipient guard as claimBondRefund above.
  assert(disclose(recipientAddr != pad(32, "")), "Recipient address cannot be empty");

  assert(
    lockedBonds.member(disclose(caller.bytes)),
    "No locked bond found for caller"
  );
  const bond = lockedBonds.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes));
  assert(disclose(bond > 0), "Bond already claimed");

  // Fix (2026-07-30, security audit): reads the governor-attested
  // REAL settlement, not the unpaid self-declaration revealBuyCommit
  // writes to dvTokensPurchased — see that ledger's own comment and
  // recordDarkVeilSettlement's comment for the full reasoning. A buyer who
  // revealed a commitment but never actually settled on Cardano has no
  // entry here, so `purchased` correctly floors at 0 (full forfeiture),
  // matching the real economic outcome instead of trusting their own claim.
  const purchased = settledDvPurchases.member(disclose(caller.bytes))
    ? settledDvPurchases.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes))
    : 0;

  assert(
    disclose(verifyRatioRefund(bond, purchased, baseSlot, claimedRefund)),
    "Claimed refund does not match the ratio-based formula"
  );

  // Clear before paying out (checks-effects-interactions).
  lockedBonds.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose(0));

  const dest = right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(disclose(UserAddress { bytes: recipientAddr }));
  sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), disclose(claimedRefund), dest);

  // Route the forfeited remainder to the platform. One destination, so no
  // share to claim and nothing to verify — the amount is fully determined by
  // the bond and the refund already checked above.
  const forfeited = (bond - claimedRefund) as Uint<128>;
  const platformDest = right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(disclose(UserAddress { bytes: platformAddr }));
  sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), disclose(forfeited), platformDest);
}

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — DARKVEIL PRIVATE BUY (from darkveil.compact)
// ============================================================================

// Submit a private buy commitment during the DarkVeil buying phase.
// The commitment hides the purchase amount and buyer identity — no payment
// happens here, deliberately: checking payment now would require
// disclosing the amount, defeating the point of committing before
// revealing.
// Phase 4 fix (2026-07-12): previously ANY caller could submit a buy
// commitment with no proof of prior DarkVeil registration at all, bypassing
// the wallet-age/allowlist/NIGHT-bond eligibility gate entirely. Fixed by
// requiring the caller to recompute their OWN registration nullifier (same
// secret + same registration nonce + bondAmount used at
// registerForDarkVeil) and prove it's already a member of
// registrationNullifiers — this proves prior registration without
// revealing WHICH registrant is buying (a nullifier is already a one-way,
// unlinkable value, and this one is already public from registration),
// preserving the commit-time identity-blindness this circuit is designed
// around.
// Fix (2026-07-21, High): nullifier is no longer a caller-supplied
// parameter — see computeBuyNullifier's own comment for the full finding
// (unlimited resubmission + precomputable identity leak) this closes.
// Fix (2026-07-30): proof of prior registration is now a real Merkle
// membership proof (verifyRegistrant) against registrantRoot, not a
// disclosed nullifier value — see verifyRegistrant's own comment for why
// the old nullifier-membership check was publicly precomputable and
// de-anonymizing.
export circuit submitBuyCommit(
  commitment: Bytes<32>,
  timestamp: Uint<64>
): [] {
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Buying), "DarkVeil buying not active");

  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  verifyRegistrant(caller.bytes);

  assert(
    !commitments.member(disclose(commitment)),
    "Commitment already exists"
  );

  const nullifier = computeBuyNullifier(sk.bytes, launchId);
  assert(
    !buyNullifiers.member(disclose(nullifier)),
    "Already bought in this DarkVeil phase"
  );
  buyNullifiers.insert(disclose(nullifier));

  commitments.insert(disclose(commitment), disclose(BuyCommit {
    commitment: commitment,
    nullifier: nullifier,
    timestamp: timestamp,
    status: CommitStatus.Open
  }));

  dvTotalParticipants.increment(1);
}

// Reveal a buy commitment (after DarkVeil closes) — this is when the
// actual purchase amount becomes public, by design.
//
// Bonus fix from this merge: revealBuyCommit now updates both
// cumulativePurchases (5% cumulative cap, previously only reachable via
// the standalone checkAndUpdateCap that nothing here ever called) and
// dvTokensPurchased (needed by claimRatioBondRefund above) atomically,
// same pattern Midnight Launch's merged revealBuyCommit already uses.
//
// This circuit records a purchase; it does not take payment, and is not
// where a Cardano Launch buyer pays. ADA is not a Midnight-native asset, so a
// Cardano Launch DarkVeil purchase is settled on Cardano: the buyer presents their
// own allocation against the anchored root and pays there, via the curve's
// ClaimDarkVeilTokens. What this circuit commits to is bounded before that
// happens — the amount revealed here cannot exceed the per-registrant
// baseSlot, nor the pool-wide dvAllocation, nor the wallet's cumulative
// cap, each asserted below — and what a buyer actually receives is decided
// by the Cardano claim against the governor-attested settlement record.
//
// Fix (2026-07-30): takes currentTimestamp now, bound to real chain
// time via blockTimeGte/blockTimeLte (same 1-hour-band idiom already used
// throughout cto_governance.compact/lp_escrow.compact) — an unbound
// caller-supplied timestamp here would let anyone forge "still within the
// reveal window" regardless of real elapsed time, defeating the deadline
// below entirely.
export circuit revealBuyCommit(
  commitment: Bytes<32>,
  tokenAmount: Uint<128>,
  pricePerToken: Uint<128>,
  currentTimestamp: Uint<64>
): [] {
  assert(blockTimeGte(disclose(currentTimestamp)), "currentTimestamp cannot be in the future");
  assert(
    blockTimeLte(disclose((currentTimestamp + 3600) as Uint<64>)),
    "currentTimestamp too far in the past"
  );

  // DarkVeil must be closed
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Closed), "DarkVeil not closed");

  // Fix (2026-07-30): reveal window closes revealWindowSeconds after
  // the real, on-chain-recorded close time (fairLaunchCert.closeTimestamp,
  // set once by closeDarkVeil) — without this, an unrevealed Open
  // commitment could sit in `commitments` forever with no expiry path.
  const revealDeadline = (fairLaunchCert.closeTimestamp + revealWindowSeconds) as Uint<64>;
  assert(disclose(currentTimestamp <= revealDeadline), "Reveal window has closed");

  // Verify commitment exists and is still Open
  assert(commitments.member(disclose(commitment)), "Commitment not found");
  const commit = commitments.lookup(disclose(commitment));
  assert(disclose(commit.status == CommitStatus.Open), "Commitment already revealed");

  // Verify the caller owns this commitment by recomputing it
  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  const nonce = getBuyNonce();
  const recomputed = computeBuyCommit(
    caller.bytes,
    launchId,
    tokenAmount,
    pricePerToken,
    nonce
  );
  assert(disclose(recomputed == commitment), "Not the commitment owner");
  // Zero extra disclosure cost — caller is already bound to this tx
  // via the commitment-ownership proof above.
  assert(disclose(caller.bytes != creatorKey), "Creator cannot buy their own DarkVeil allocation");

  // Verify the price matches DarkVeil price
  assert(disclose(pricePerToken == dvPrice), "Price mismatch with DarkVeil price");

  // Design requirement: per-registrant cap — bounds a
  // single registrant to their fair share of the pool rather than the
  // entire dvAllocation.
  assert(disclose(tokenAmount <= baseSlot), "Exceeds per-registrant DarkVeil allocation");

  // Verify tokens don't exceed the pool-wide allocation
  const newTotal = (totalTokensCommitted + tokenAmount) as Uint<128>;
  assert(disclose(newTotal <= dvAllocation), "Exceeds DarkVeil allocation");

  // Bonus fix (this merge): enforce the 5% cumulative wallet cap — same
  // map, same walletCap, same identity checkAndUpdateCap uses.
  const priorCumulative = cumulativePurchases.member(disclose(caller.bytes))
    ? cumulativePurchases.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes))
    : 0;
  const newCumulative = (priorCumulative + tokenAmount) as Uint<128>;
  assert(disclose(newCumulative <= walletCap), "Purchase exceeds 5% wallet cap");
  cumulativePurchases.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose(newCumulative));

  // Bonus fix (this merge): track this buyer's DarkVeil-only purchase
  // total, needed by claimRatioBondRefund.
  const currentDvPurchased = dvTokensPurchased.member(disclose(caller.bytes))
    ? dvTokensPurchased.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes))
    : 0;
  dvTokensPurchased.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose((currentDvPurchased + tokenAmount) as Uint<128>));

  // Update aggregate stats
  totalTokensCommitted = disclose(newTotal);
  const raisedF = ((tokenAmount as Field) * (pricePerToken as Field)) as Field;
  const raised = raisedF as Uint<128>;
  totalRaisedCommitted = disclose((totalRaisedCommitted + raised) as Uint<128>);

  // Mark commitment as revealed
  commitments.insert(disclose(commitment), disclose(BuyCommit {
    commitment: commit.commitment,
    nullifier: commit.nullifier,
    timestamp: commit.timestamp,
    status: CommitStatus.Revealed
  }));
}

// Cancel a buy commitment (before DarkVeil closes)
// Only the commitment owner can cancel
export circuit cancelBuyCommit(commitment: Bytes<32>): [] {
  // Must be in Buying phase
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Buying), "Can only cancel during buying phase");

  // Verify commitment exists
  assert(commitments.member(disclose(commitment)), "Commitment not found");
  const commit = commitments.lookup(disclose(commitment));
  assert(disclose(commit.status == CommitStatus.Open), "Commitment not open");

  // Verify ownership via the nullifier (derived from the secret key —
  // Fix, 2026-07-21, must match submitBuyCommit's derivation).
  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const expectedNullifier = computeBuyNullifier(sk.bytes, launchId);
  assert(disclose(expectedNullifier == commit.nullifier), "Not the commitment owner");

  // Mark as cancelled
  commitments.insert(disclose(commitment), disclose(BuyCommit {
    commitment: commit.commitment,
    nullifier: commit.nullifier,
    timestamp: commit.timestamp,
    status: CommitStatus.Cancelled
  }));

  // Remove from nullifier set so the user can buy again
  buyNullifiers.remove(disclose(commit.nullifier));

  // Decrement participant count
  dvTotalParticipants.decrement(1);
}

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — ZK FAIR LAUNCH CERTIFICATE
// ============================================================================

// Close DarkVeil and generate the ZK Fair Launch Certificate
// Governor only — called after the buying phase ends
//
// Also takes `baseSlot_`, the per-registrant DarkVeil allocation, computed
// off-chain by the governor from `dvAllocation / registrationCount` at
// this exact moment — the final registrant count is only known once
// registration is over.
export circuit closeDarkVeil(closeTimestamp: Uint<64>, baseSlot_: Uint<128>): [] {
  // Must be in Buying phase
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Buying), "DarkVeil not in buying phase");

  // Verify governor
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can close DarkVeil");

  assert(disclose(baseSlot_ > 0), "baseSlot must be positive");
  // Fix (2026-07-30): verifyRatioRefund's own 2^40-1 ceiling on
  // `allocated` (baseSlot) would otherwise only surface the first time
  // claimRatioBondRefund runs, after every registrant has already bonded
  // and bought — fail fast here instead, before any bond refund can be
  // blocked.
  assert(disclose(baseSlot_ <= 1099511627775), "baseSlot exceeds verifyRatioRefund's safe range"); // 2^40 - 1
  // Fix (2026-07-21, Medium): baseSlot_ was previously only checked
  // for being positive — a governor mistake (or malicious value) could set
  // it so high that baseSlot * registrationCount collectively promises far
  // more tokens than dvAllocation actually reserves, letting early
  // revealBuyCommit callers (bounded individually by baseSlot, per-call)
  // drain the pool before later ones can claim their fair share. Verified
  // via Field multiplication (avoids the non-Field multiplication
  // result-width ceiling — same idiom as revealBuyCommit's raisedF below).
  const totalAllocatedF = ((baseSlot_ as Field) * (registrationCount.read() as Field)) as Field;
  const totalAllocated = totalAllocatedF as Uint<128>;
  assert(disclose(totalAllocated <= dvAllocation), "baseSlot * registrationCount exceeds dvAllocation");
  baseSlot = disclose(baseSlot_);

  // Transition to Closed
  dvState = DarkVeilState.Closed;

  // Generate certificate hash
  const certHash = computeCertHash(
    launchId,
    dvTotalParticipants.read(),
    totalTokensCommitted,
    totalRaisedCommitted,
    closeTimestamp
  );

  // Store the certificate
  fairLaunchCert = disclose(FairLaunchCert {
    launchId: launchId,
    totalParticipants: dvTotalParticipants.read(),
    totalTokensAllocated: totalTokensCommitted,
    totalRaised: totalRaisedCommitted,
    participationRate: 0, // Computed off-chain from raw numbers
    closeTimestamp: closeTimestamp,
    certHash: certHash
  });
}

// The ZK Fair Launch Certificate is `fairLaunchCert` in this contract's
// public state, read straight from chain. That is what makes it verifiable
// by anyone rather than only by whoever can afford to call a circuit.

// Fix (2026-07-30, security audit): governor-only — records that
// `buyerKey` really settled `settledAmount` tokens for real ADA, observed
// from a real Cardano ClaimDarkVeilTokens transaction.
// This is the ONLY way settledDvPurchases is ever written. The off-chain
// caller (a relayer/CLI watching Cardano for real ClaimDarkVeilTokens
// events) is expected to call this once per real settlement, with
// settledAmount taken directly from that transaction's own dv_amount, not
// from anything the buyer separately claims on Midnight. Idempotent
// overwrite (insert, not add) — safe because a buyer can only ever settle
// once on Cardano (the Merkle-nullifier claim mechanism prevents a
// double-claim there), so this should only ever be called once per buyer
// per launch in practice; a governor mistake re-calling it just overwrites
// with the same (or a corrected) real value rather than double-counting.
export circuit recordDarkVeilSettlement(
  buyerKey: Bytes<32>,
  settledAmount: Uint<128>
): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can record a DarkVeil settlement");

  // Same per-registrant ceiling revealBuyCommit itself enforces on the
  // (untrusted) declared amount — a real settlement can never exceed it
  // either, since Cardano's own ClaimDarkVeilTokens is gated by the same
  // baseSlot-derived Merkle allocation.
  assert(disclose(settledAmount <= baseSlot), "Settlement exceeds per-registrant DarkVeil allocation");

  // Once finalized, the settlement record is closed. sweepForfeitedBond reads
  // it to decide that a registrant never settled, so it must not be able to
  // change after a sweep could have acted on it.
  assert(disclose(!settlementFinalized), "DarkVeil settlement is already finalized");

  settledDvPurchases.insert(disclose(buyerKey), disclose(settledAmount));
}

// Close the settlement record. Governor-only, one-way, and callable only on a
// normally-closed DarkVeil.
//
// This is the governor attesting that the Cardano claim window has ended and
// every real ClaimDarkVeilTokens settlement has been recorded. Compact cannot
// observe Cardano, so that remains an attested fact — the same trust model as
// the allowlist root, the CTO balance snapshot and the staking snapshot.
//
// A launch where the claim window never actually ran must NOT be finalized:
// registrants would be recorded as non-settlers through no fault of their own.
// cancelDarkVeil/markDarkVeilFailed is the correct path there, and it routes
// every bond back in full via claimBondRefund.
export circuit finalizeDvSettlement(): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can finalize DarkVeil settlement");

  assert(
    disclose(phase != LaunchPhase.Cancelled && !dvFailed && dvState == DarkVeilState.Closed),
    "Only finalizable after a normally-closed, non-cancelled, non-failed DarkVeil"
  );
  assert(disclose(!settlementFinalized), "DarkVeil settlement is already finalized");

  settlementFinalized = disclose(true);
}

// Collect a bond that was forfeited in full, and pay it to the platform.
//
// A registrant who bonded, revealed a commitment on Midnight, and then never
// settled on Cardano forfeits their whole bond. That much the refund path
// already enforces: claimRatioBondRefund reads the governor-attested
// settledDvPurchases, so their refund computes to zero. What it cannot do is
// MOVE the NIGHT — it only ever runs when the registrant calls it, and a
// registrant owed nothing never will. Their bond is real (registerForDarkVeil
// takes custody via receiveUnshielded) and would otherwise sit in this
// contract's balance permanently. This circuit is the platform's own door to
// it, making the same sendUnshielded call the refund path already makes.
//
// Deliberately PERMISSIONLESS. The destination is sealed at deploy and the
// amount is fully determined by the ledger, so calling it confers nothing on
// the caller — the same "the condition is the authorization" idiom as the
// Cardano validators' ExpireCurve and Graduate. Not requiring the governor
// means collection cannot stall on one key.
//
// Deliberately FULL FORFEITURE ONLY. A registrant who settled in part is owed
// a real refund and has every reason to claim it themselves; sweeping their
// bond would take money that is theirs. Requiring a zero settlement keeps this
// circuit unable to touch anything anyone is owed.
//
// Deliberately PROVEN. The bond swept must belong to a key with a real path in
// registrantRoot. lockedBonds is written by each registrant's own call and
// registrantRoot is published later by one key, so the two are separate
// records of the same set with separate authors; requiring both to agree means
// a bond is only ever collected here on evidence from each of them. See the
// REGISTRANT EXCLUSION DISPUTE section below for the other half.
export circuit sweepForfeitedBond(registrantKey: Bytes<32>): [] {
  assert(
    disclose(phase != LaunchPhase.Cancelled && !dvFailed && dvState == DarkVeilState.Closed),
    "Only sweepable after a normally-closed, non-cancelled, non-failed DarkVeil"
  );
  assert(disclose(settlementFinalized), "DarkVeil settlement is not finalized yet");

  assert(lockedBonds.member(disclose(registrantKey)), "No locked bond found for registrant");
  const bond = lockedBonds.lookup(disclose(registrantKey));
  assert(disclose(bond > 0), "Bond already claimed or swept");

  // Absent or zero both mean the same thing: nothing was ever settled for this
  // registrant, so nothing is owed back to them.
  const settled = settledDvPurchases.member(disclose(registrantKey))
    ? settledDvPurchases.lookup(disclose(registrantKey))
    : 0;
  assert(disclose(settled == 0), "Registrant settled part of their allocation and is owed a refund");

  // The published tree must actually contain this registrant. Last, because it
  // is the expensive check and the cheap ones above already name the common
  // refusals.
  verifyRegistrant(registrantKey);

  // Clear before paying out — same checks-effects-interactions order as both
  // refund circuits.
  lockedBonds.insert(disclose(registrantKey), disclose(0));

  const platformDest = right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(disclose(UserAddress { bytes: platformAddr }));
  sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), disclose(bond), platformDest);
}

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — REGISTRANT EXCLUSION DISPUTE
// ============================================================================
//
// What decides where a bond ends up is the record the registrant wrote
// themselves.
//
// There are two records of who registered, and they have different authors.
// lockedBonds is written a row at a time by each registrant's own
// registerForDarkVeil call. registrantRoot is one hash, published later, by
// one key, at the registration freeze; it exists so a buyer can prove
// membership without naming themselves, and it is meant to commit to exactly
// the set lockedBonds already holds. A Merkle root, though, carries no
// evidence of which set it was built from, and buying is gated on it.
//
// These circuits make the two records answer to each other:
//
//   - A bond is only ever collected against a real published path
//     (sweepForfeitedBond above), so a row in lockedBonds with no leaf in the
//     tree is worth nothing to anyone.
//   - A registrant holding a real bond may dispute, which starts a fixed
//     window.
//   - Anyone at all may end that window by producing the disputant's path.
//     The tree is public, so a registrant the tree really does contain can be
//     answered by any observer — not only by the key that published the root.
//
// So the two outcomes separate cleanly. A registrant who simply bought nothing
// is in the tree, is answered, and forfeits on the ordinary terms. A
// registrant with no path has nothing that can be produced against them, and
// takes their bond back in full.
//
// Identity: disputing discloses caller.bytes, exactly as both refund circuits
// already do. lockedBonds has carried that key publicly since registration and
// buying is long over by this point, so this spends no privacy the refund path
// does not spend already.

// Open a dispute: a bond is held for this caller, and they hold no path in
// registrantRoot.
//
// Available from the DarkVeil close rather than from settlement finalization,
// so the window runs alongside the Cardano claim window instead of after it.
// The payout waits for finalization regardless — see claimDisputedBond.
export circuit disputeRegistrantExclusion(currentTimestamp: Uint<64>): [] {
  // Same one-hour band on a caller-supplied timestamp as revealBuyCommit —
  // without it, the window below could be declared already elapsed.
  assert(blockTimeGte(disclose(currentTimestamp)), "currentTimestamp cannot be in the future");
  assert(
    blockTimeLte(disclose((currentTimestamp + 3600) as Uint<64>)),
    "currentTimestamp too far in the past"
  );

  assert(
    disclose(phase != LaunchPhase.Cancelled && !dvFailed && dvState == DarkVeilState.Closed),
    "Only disputable after a normally-closed, non-cancelled, non-failed DarkVeil"
  );

  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  assert(disclose(!isKeyZero(caller)), "Invalid user key");

  assert(lockedBonds.member(disclose(caller.bytes)), "No locked bond found for caller");
  const bond = lockedBonds.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes));
  assert(disclose(bond > 0), "Bond already claimed or swept");

  // Answered first, open second. An answered dispute is still an open one —
  // rebutRegistrantExclusion records the answer rather than clearing the
  // dispute — so checking the other order would leave this unreachable and
  // report the vaguer of the two refusals.
  assert(
    !rebuttedExclusions.member(disclose(caller.bytes)),
    "This registrant's membership in the tree has already been shown"
  );
  assert(!disputedExclusions.member(disclose(caller.bytes)), "Exclusion already disputed");

  const claimableFrom = (currentTimestamp + exclusionDisputeWindow) as Uint<64>;
  disputedExclusions.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose(claimableFrom));
}

// Answer a dispute by producing the disputant's own path in registrantRoot.
//
// Permissionless, and deliberately so: the tree is published, so whoever holds
// it can answer, and collection never depends on one key staying available.
// The proof is the entire authorization — it recomputes the published root or
// the transaction fails — so calling this confers nothing on the caller.
export circuit rebutRegistrantExclusion(registrantKey: Bytes<32>): [] {
  assert(disputedExclusions.member(disclose(registrantKey)), "No open dispute for this registrant");
  assert(!rebuttedExclusions.member(disclose(registrantKey)), "Dispute already answered");

  verifyRegistrant(registrantKey);

  rebuttedExclusions.insert(disclose(registrantKey));
}

// Take back a bond behind a dispute that stood unanswered for the full window.
//
// Gated on settlementFinalized for the same reason sweepForfeitedBond is: the
// settlement record has to be closed before anything reads it to decide that
// nothing is owed, or a settlement recorded afterwards would mean this paid
// out in full where a ratio refund was owed.
export circuit claimDisputedBond(
  recipientAddr: Bytes<32>,
  currentTimestamp: Uint<64>
): [] {
  assert(blockTimeGte(disclose(currentTimestamp)), "currentTimestamp cannot be in the future");
  assert(
    blockTimeLte(disclose((currentTimestamp + 3600) as Uint<64>)),
    "currentTimestamp too far in the past"
  );

  assert(disclose(settlementFinalized), "DarkVeil settlement is not finalized yet");
  // Same empty-recipient guard as both refund circuits.
  assert(disclose(recipientAddr != pad(32, "")), "Recipient address cannot be empty");

  const sk = getUserSecret();
  const caller = deriveUserPublicKey(sk, launchId);
  assert(disclose(!isKeyZero(caller)), "Invalid user key");

  assert(disputedExclusions.member(disclose(caller.bytes)), "No open dispute for this registrant");
  assert(
    !rebuttedExclusions.member(disclose(caller.bytes)),
    "This registrant's membership in the tree was shown; the bond forfeits on the ordinary terms"
  );

  const claimableFrom = disputedExclusions.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes));
  assert(disclose(currentTimestamp >= claimableFrom), "Dispute window has not elapsed");

  // A registrant who settled was never excluded — the ratio refund is theirs
  // to claim and pays them what they are actually owed.
  const settled = settledDvPurchases.member(disclose(caller.bytes))
    ? settledDvPurchases.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes))
    : 0;
  assert(disclose(settled == 0), "Registrant settled part of their allocation; claim the ratio refund instead");

  assert(lockedBonds.member(disclose(caller.bytes)), "No locked bond found for caller");
  const bond = lockedBonds.lookup(disclose(caller.bytes));
  assert(disclose(bond > 0), "Bond already claimed or swept");

  // Clear before paying out — same checks-effects-interactions order as every
  // other circuit here that moves NIGHT.
  lockedBonds.insert(disclose(caller.bytes), disclose(0));

  const dest = right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(disclose(UserAddress { bytes: recipientAddr }));
  sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), disclose(bond), dest);
}

// ============================================================================
// VIEW / READ — served by the public ledger, not by circuits
// ============================================================================
//
// `dvState`, `totalTokensCommitted`, `totalRaisedCommitted`, `dvAllocation`,
// `dvPrice`, `fairLaunchCert` and `cumulativePurchases` are all exported
// ledger fields, so the indexer serves them to any reader at no cost and with
// no transaction. Every exported circuit, by contrast, carries its verifier
// key in this contract's on-chain state, and a deploy writes that state within
// one block's write budget. Keeping reads out of the circuit set spends that
// budget only on the circuits that need it.
//
// Read these off-chain through the generated `ledger()` accessor. Add an
// exported circuit only for something that changes state or proves something
// in zero knowledge.

// ============================================================================
// CIRCUITS — ADMIN / GOVERNOR
// ============================================================================

// Update allowlist root, for late additions during the registration window.
//
// Bounded to exactly the window where the root can still admit anyone:
// registerForDarkVeil is the only circuit in this contract that reads
// allowlistRoot, and it requires this same phase and sub-phase pair. Once
// registration freezes, the registrant set is fixed for the round and
// startBuying publishes registrantRoot over it — so from that moment the
// allowlist decides nothing, and this circuit says so rather than leaving a
// live write path into a field the rest of the launch still reads.
//
// The same guarantee stated from the registrant's side: the tree you proved
// membership in to register is the tree that was in force for the whole
// registration window, and nothing can replace it afterwards.
// Threshold attestation (2026-08-11): one call carries one attestor. The root
// is written once `allowlistThreshold` DISTINCT attestors have called for the
// same root inside one live round.
//
// `currentTimestamp` is new and exists for the expiry: partial approvals
// persist between calls, and the registration window is longer than the expiry
// they are bounded by, so a phase gate alone would leave a half-approval live
// for the whole window. Bound to real chain time the same way every other
// timestamp-taking circuit here is.
export circuit updateAllowlistRoot(newRoot: Bytes<32>, currentTimestamp: Uint<64>): [] {
  const attestorSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const attestorKey = deriveGovernorKey(attestorSecret);
  const isAllowlistAttestor1 = disclose(attestorKey.bytes == allowlistAttestor1);
  const isAllowlistAttestor2 = disclose(attestorKey.bytes == allowlistAttestor2);
  const isAllowlistAttestor3 = disclose(attestorKey.bytes == allowlistAttestor3);
  assert(
    isAllowlistAttestor1 || isAllowlistAttestor2 || isAllowlistAttestor3,
    "Only a registered attestor can update allowlist"
  );
  assert(disclose(phase == LaunchPhase.DarkVeil), "Allowlist is fixed outside the DarkVeil phase");
  assert(
    disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Registration),
    "Allowlist is fixed once registration freezes"
  );
  assert(blockTimeGte(disclose(currentTimestamp)), "currentTimestamp cannot be in the future");
  assert(
    blockTimeLte(disclose((currentTimestamp + 3600) as Uint<64>)),
    "currentTimestamp too far in the past"
  );

  const allowlistExpired =
    currentTimestamp > (pendingAllowlistOpenedAt + allowlistExpirySeconds) as Uint<64>;
  const sameAllowlistPending = disclose(pendingAllowlistRoot == newRoot);
  if (disclose(!sameAllowlistPending || allowlistExpired)) {
    allowlistRound.increment(1);
    pendingAllowlistRoot = disclose(newRoot);
    pendingAllowlistOpenedAt = disclose(currentTimestamp);
  }

  const allowlistRoundNow = allowlistRound.read();
  allowlistApprovals.insert(disclose(attestorKey.bytes), disclose(allowlistRoundNow));

  const c1 =
    allowlistApprovals.member(allowlistAttestor1) &&
    allowlistApprovals.lookup(allowlistAttestor1) == allowlistRoundNow;
  const c2 =
    allowlistApprovals.member(allowlistAttestor2) &&
    allowlistApprovals.lookup(allowlistAttestor2) == allowlistRoundNow;
  const c3 =
    allowlistApprovals.member(allowlistAttestor3) &&
    allowlistApprovals.lookup(allowlistAttestor3) == allowlistRoundNow;
  const allowlistApprovalCount = ((c1 ? 1 : 0) + (c2 ? 1 : 0) + (c3 ? 1 : 0)) as Uint<8>;

  if (disclose(allowlistApprovalCount >= allowlistThreshold)) {
    allowlistRoot = disclose(newRoot);
  }
}

// Record that DarkVeil failed (<50% participation).
// Independent of `phase` so claimBondRefund keeps working regardless of
// what phase the launch is in afterward. One-way: DarkVeil can only fail
// once per launch.
export circuit markDarkVeilFailed(): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can mark DarkVeil failed");
  assert(disclose(!dvFailed), "DarkVeil already marked failed");
  // A normally-closed DarkVeil is not a failed one: claimRatioBondRefund
  // already treats the two as mutually exclusive, and marking a closed
  // phase failed would open the full-refund path to registrants whose
  // bonds are meant to settle against what they actually bought.
  assert(disclose(dvState != DarkVeilState.Closed), "DarkVeil already closed normally");
  dvFailed = disclose(true);
}

// Advance to next phase
export circuit advancePhase(newPhase: LaunchPhase): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can advance phase");

  // Fix (2026-07-30): the four checks below each correctly guard
  // their own forward transition (DarkVeil/Public/Graduated all assert the
  // one valid prior phase; Cancelled is reachable from either), but NONE
  // of them ever matches newPhase == Pending — so nothing stopped the
  // governor from calling advancePhase(Pending) from ANY state (DarkVeil,
  // Public, even Graduated), silently resetting the whole one-way
  // lifecycle backwards with no assertion firing at all. Pending is only
  // ever the constructor's own initial value, never a legitimate target.
  assert(disclose(newPhase != LaunchPhase.Pending), "Cannot advance phase back to Pending");

  // Phase transitions must be sequential
  if (disclose(newPhase == LaunchPhase.DarkVeil)) {
    assert(disclose(phase == LaunchPhase.Pending), "Must be in Pending to start DarkVeil");
  }
  if (disclose(newPhase == LaunchPhase.Public)) {
    assert(disclose(phase == LaunchPhase.DarkVeil), "Must be in DarkVeil to start Public");
  }
  if (disclose(newPhase == LaunchPhase.Graduated)) {
    assert(disclose(phase == LaunchPhase.Public), "Must be in Public to graduate");
  }
  if (disclose(newPhase == LaunchPhase.Cancelled)) {
    // Can cancel from DarkVeil or Public, but not from Graduated
    assert(disclose(phase != LaunchPhase.Graduated), "Cannot cancel after graduation");
  }

  phase = disclose(newPhase);
}

// --- from darkveil.compact: dvState sub-phase admin circuits ---

// Start registration phase
export circuit startRegistration(): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can start");
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Inactive), "DarkVeil already started");
  dvState = DarkVeilState.Registration;
}

// Start buying phase
// Fix (2026-07-30): now takes registrantRoot_, published by the
// governor at this exact freeze point — the moment registrationCount stops
// growing (the registration-freeze invariant), so the registrant set
// this root commits to is final for the round. The governor computes the
// tree off-chain from the real, already-public registrant set (readable
// from lockedBonds/registrationNullifiers — same off-chain-computed,
// governor-published-root trust model already used for allowlistRoot and
// every other Merkle root on this platform), not a new trust assumption.
export circuit startBuying(registrantRoot_: Bytes<32>): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can start buying");
  assert(disclose(dvState == DarkVeilState.Registration), "Must be in registration phase");
  // Refuse to open buying below the minimum registrant floor — the
  // governor must call cancelDarkVeil() instead, which is fully refundable.
  assert(
    disclose(registrationCount.read() >= minDvParticipants),
    "Below minimum DarkVeil participant threshold — cancel instead"
  );
  registrantRoot = disclose(registrantRoot_);
  dvState = DarkVeilState.Buying;
}

// Give up on a DarkVeil phase that has stopped moving, without asking the
// key that stopped moving it.
//
// Every transition through this phase — startRegistration, startBuying,
// closeDarkVeil, advancePhase, activateCurve — is governor-only. A governor
// who goes silent partway through therefore freezes the launch with every
// bond locked inside it, and the one existing timeout (expireCurve) sits
// behind activateCurve, which is itself governor-only. So there was no point
// before the public phase at which anyone else could act.
//
// This is the missing one. Permissionless, because a hatch that needs the
// governor is not a hatch: the deadline is the entire authorization, the same
// idiom expireCurve already uses. Calling it confers nothing — it cannot move
// value, only mark the phase failed, which is what makes every locked bond
// claimable through the refund path that already exists.
//
// The deadline is measured from registrationCloseTime, which is sealed at
// deploy and therefore fixed before anyone bonds. That is the honest bound on
// this: the governor chooses that timestamp, so they can set it far out and
// push the hatch out with it. What they cannot do is move it afterwards, and
// it is public, so a registrant can see the number before deciding to bond.
//
// The grace period on top is deliberately generous. Registration close leaves
// only the buying window and settlement to run, so a week is several times the
// remaining legitimate sequence — this should never fire on a governor who is
// merely slow.
export circuit expireDarkVeil(): [] {
  // Only a phase that is still in flight. A closed DarkVeil settles through
  // the ratio refund, and a cancelled one is already claimable.
  assert(
    disclose(dvState != DarkVeilState.Closed && dvState != DarkVeilState.Cancelled),
    "DarkVeil already closed or cancelled"
  );

  // Real chain time, never a caller-supplied parameter compared against the
  // deadline — a bare argument would let any caller name a large value and
  // expire a healthy launch on demand.
  assert(
    blockTimeGt((registrationCloseTime + darkVeilExpirySeconds) as Uint<64>),
    "DarkVeil has not yet exceeded its expiry deadline"
  );

  dvState = DarkVeilState.Cancelled;
  dvFailed = disclose(true);
  // Terminal, so a governor who wakes up afterwards cannot carry on with a
  // launch whose registrants have already been refunded.
  phase = LaunchPhase.Cancelled;
}

// Cancel DarkVeil (failure path).
// All bonds are refundable via claimBondRefund above.
//
// `dvFailed` is set here as well as `dvState`, and both are required for
// that refund promise to hold. claimBondRefund gates on
// `phase == Cancelled || dvFailed`, and claimRatioBondRefund gates on
// `dvState == Closed` — so setting `dvState = Cancelled` alone satisfies
// NEITHER path and every bond would be stranded. This is the escape hatch
// startBuying() points the governor at when registration is below
// minDvParticipants, so it must actually release the bonds it promises.
export circuit cancelDarkVeil(): [] {
  const govSecret = getGovernorSecret();
  const govKey = deriveGovernorKey(govSecret);
  assert(disclose(govKey.bytes == governorKey), "Only governor can cancel");
  assert(disclose(dvState != DarkVeilState.Closed), "Already closed");
  assert(disclose(dvState != DarkVeilState.Cancelled), "Already cancelled");
  dvState = DarkVeilState.Cancelled;
  dvFailed = disclose(true);
}

// ============================================================================
// PRIVACY ANALYSIS
// ============================================================================
//
// What goes ON-CHAIN (public ledger):
//   - launchId, allowlistRoot, phase, totalSupply, maxWalletPercent, bondAmount
//   - walletCap (precomputed)
//   - governorKey, creatorKey (derived, not raw secrets)
//   - platformAddr (real unshielded address, fixed at deploy)
//   - cumulativePurchases (keyed by UserPublicKey, not real identity)
//   - lockedBonds, dvTokensPurchased (keyed by UserPublicKey)
//   - registrationNullifiers, buyNullifiers (commitment hashes, not
//     linkable to identity)
//   - registrationCount, dvTotalParticipants (aggregate counters)
//   - dvState, commitments (commitment hashes + nullifiers — not linkable
//     to identity until revealed)
//   - dvAllocation, dvPrice, allowlistSize, registrationCloseTime (sealed)
//   - totalTokensCommitted, totalRaisedCommitted (public for transparency
//     once revealed)
//   - baseSlot (per-registrant allocation, set once at DarkVeil close)
//   - fairLaunchCert (public certificate, generated on close)
//   - disputedExclusions, rebuttedExclusions (keyed by UserPublicKey). A
//     dispute names its own caller, which is the same disclosure both refund
//     circuits already make and the same key lockedBonds has carried publicly
//     since registration — and it is only reachable once buying is over.
//
// What stays PRIVATE (never leaves the prover):
//   - User's secret key (getUserSecret witness)
//   - Merkle proof (getMerkleProof witness)
//   - Buy nonce (getBuyNonce witness)
//   - Governor's secret key (getGovernorSecret witness)
//   - Individual buy amounts (hidden in commitments until revealed)
//   - Mapping from UserPublicKey to real-world identity
//
// Privacy guarantees:
//   - Allowlist membership is proven via ZK without revealing which entry,
//     and the leaf is bound to the caller's own identity  so it
//     can't be borrowed from another registrant's proof.
//   - UserPublicKey is a domain-separated hash of the user's secret — not
//     linkable to their real-world identity or to other launches.
//   - Registration/buy nullifiers are unique per (user, launch[, nonce]) —
//     prevent double-registration/double-buying without revealing which
//     user acted.
//   - DarkVeil buy amounts are committed privately; only the aggregate
//     (totalTokensCommitted/totalRaisedCommitted) is public until an
//     individual reveal, and even then the mapping from UserPublicKey to
//     real identity stays unknown.
//   - Cumulative purchases are visible by UserPublicKey, but the mapping
//     from UserPublicKey to real identity is unknown.
//
// ============================================================================
